// Chapter 7: a six-node cluster across failure domains. The deployment sequence
// is chapter 1's, with one label on every Node, one field on the discovery run,
// and one flag plus one field per group on the draft. What the chapter is
// about is the layout rule the operator enforces on behalf of the control
// plane, why the fleet the training was commissioned for, six nodes in two
// cabinets, is refused by it, and what the operator writes and holds once a
// cluster is in failure-domain mode.
TRAINING.register({
  id: 'failure-domains',
  title: 'A six-node cluster across failure domains',
  summary: 'Six OpenShift workers in three cabinets, labeled, discovered, and deployed in failure-domain mode, and the two-cabinet layout the operator refuses, read from its own refusal message.',
  goal: 'you can label a fleet\'s fault groups, write a discovery run that reads them, judge from the stripe whether a layout will activate before approving it, and read what the operator writes onto the cluster and its nodes afterward.',

  sections: [
    {
      heading: 'Why three cabinets, and not two',
      body: [
        'This chapter was commissioned as a six-node OpenShift cluster in two cabinets. The operator refuses that layout, so the chapter deploys the six nodes in three cabinets of two, and this section is why. Failure-domain mode asks the control plane to keep the chunks of one stripe in independent fault groups, and the control plane accepts a cluster for activation only when the groups can carry that: at least the stripe\'s parity plus two distinct domains, with the same number of hosts in every one. The operator mirrors that gate from sbcli in `erasurecoding.FailureDomainViolation`, so the refusal lands while the document can still be edited rather than at activation, when it cannot.',
        'For the default `1+1` stripe, parity plus two is three. Two cabinets pass the count only with a `1+0` stripe, which has no parity chunk and survives nothing, and a cluster that survives nothing has no use for failure domains. Six nodes in three cabinets of two is the smallest layout that keeps `1+1`.',
        { table: { head: ['Stripe', 'Parity', 'Domains needed', 'Six nodes'], rows: [
          ['`1+0`', '0', '2', 'Two cabinets of three, and no failure survived'],
          ['`1+1`, `2+1`, `4+1`', '1', '3', 'Three cabinets of two, this chapter'],
          ['`1+2`, `2+2`, `4+2`', '2', '4', 'Not with six nodes: four domains of equal size need eight'],
        ] } },
        'The rule has a reason on each side. Parity plus one domains is enough to place a stripe, but with one domain down there is nowhere to relocate onto, so an add or a remove after that loss has no spare domain, and the code\'s own comment states that two domains are not supported at any parity. Equal host counts are required at activation only: a cluster that has activated may grow and shrink within one host per domain, which the last section covers.',
        '**A cabinet is not an operator term.** The operator knows failure domains, and a failure domain is a label: the value of a Node label the discovery run reads, carried into the draft, the StorageNode, and the cluster\'s status as itself. `cabinet-a` is what this fleet calls one, `rack-b` is what the design documents use, and a zone name is what a cloud fleet gets. The code treats all three the same.',
        { callout: 'The operator supplies each node\'s domain index and enforces the layout. Where the chunks of a stripe land within those domains is the control plane\'s placement, which this repository does not describe beyond the rule above, and neither does this chapter.', kind: 'info' },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'A failure domain is a label value',
      body: [
        'A failure domain takes the shape of a Kubernetes label value: at most 63 characters of letters, digits, `-`, `_`, and `.`, starting and ending with a letter or digit. The design chose a label over an index because `rack-b` says what two nodes share and `1` does not, and the control plane\'s own field is the index, so the operator keeps a mapping from one to the other on the cluster.',
        { table: { head: ['Field', 'What it holds', 'Who writes it'], rows: [
          ['Node label, here `cabinet`', 'The fault group, `cabinet-a`', 'The administrator, before discovery'],
          ['`ClusterDeploymentConfig` `nodeSets[].groups[].failureDomain`', 'One label per group', 'The discovery run, then the reviewer'],
          ['`ClusterDeploymentConfig` `cluster.enableFailureDomains`', 'The mode, immutable on the cluster it lands on', 'The run, or the reviewer'],
          ['`StorageCluster` `status.failureDomains[]`', 'Each label and the index the control plane knows it by', 'The expansion, in `CreatingNodes`'],
          ['`StorageNode` `spec.config.failureDomain`', 'The node\'s label, immutable once set', 'The expansion, or a hand that fills an empty one'],
          ['`StorageNode` `status.failureDomain`', 'The index the control plane assigned, as a string', 'The node controller, from the control plane'],
        ] } },
        'The label key is the fleet\'s choice. The run reads `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` by default, which a cloud installer sets and a rack does not have, so a fleet that labels cabinets names its own key on the run. Any key up to 317 characters is accepted, and a plain `cabinet` is as good as a prefixed one.',
        { code: 'oc label node worker-0 worker-1 cabinet=cabinet-a\noc label node worker-2 worker-3 cabinet=cabinet-b\noc label node worker-4 worker-5 cabinet=cabinet-c\noc get nodes -L cabinet', title: 'Labeling the six workers' },
        'A label that is a number is a valid domain and claims its own number as the index when that number is free, because before the mapping existed a numeric label was sent to the control plane as that number. Every other label is indexed in name order from the lowest free index, so `cabinet-a`, `cabinet-b`, and `cabinet-c` become `0`, `1`, and `2`.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Discovery: reading the label',
      body: [
        'The run is chapter 1\'s with two fields under `spec.discover`. `failureDomainLabel` names the Node label the run reads every worker\'s domain from, and `enableFailureDomains` states the mode. Without the second, the run decides: the draft proposes the mode on when every worker carries a label in a layout the control plane accepts, and off otherwise, with a `ConfigWritten` event on the run saying why. Stated `true`, the draft carries the mode on even when the layout would be refused, and the event says the control plane will refuse to activate it until the topology provides enough domains. Stated `false`, the run reads no topology at all and groups the fleet by hardware alone.',
        'The run reads the label off each worker\'s Node object. A worker without it gets no domain, and when other workers do have one the run raises a `TopologyIncomplete` warning naming the unlabeled workers and proposes the mode off. A value the field cannot hold, such as one with a space, raises `FailureDomainInvalid` naming the worker, and that worker gets none. Both are corrected by labeling the node and running discovery again, since a finished run is not repeated.',
        'The domain is part of a group\'s signature. Workers that hand over the same devices at the same addresses are one group, and workers in different domains are never one group however alike their hardware, so a fleet of six identical machines in three cabinets comes out of the run as three groups of two, each carrying its cabinet in `failureDomain`.',
        'The chart has `discovery.enableFailureDomains` and no value for the label, so its own run reads the zone label only. On a cabinet-labeled fleet the chart\'s run is turned off and the run is written by hand.',
        { code: 'oc get oops cabinets -n simplyblock -w\noc get events -n simplyblock --field-selector involvedObject.name=cabinets', title: 'Following the run and reading its notes' },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'Helm values with the chart\'s run turned off', file: 'examples/failure-domains/01-helm-values.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'enabled: false', text: 'The chart\'s run would read `topology.kubernetes.io/zone`, which these nodes do not carry. `discovery.enableFailureDomains` exists in the chart, `failureDomainLabel` does not.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The discovery run', file: 'examples/failure-domains/02-operatorops-discover.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'failureDomainLabel: cabinet', text: 'The Node label the run reads. The default is `topology.kubernetes.io/zone`, and the value is copied verbatim into each group\'s `failureDomain`.' },
          { at: 'enableFailureDomains: true', text: 'States the mode. Left unset, the run proposes it on only when every worker carries the label in a layout the control plane accepts.' },
          { at: 'name: cabinets', text: 'The draft is `discovered-cabinets` and the proposed cluster `discovered-cabinets-cluster`.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Reviewing the draft: one group per cabinet',
      body: [
        'The draft carries `cluster.enableFailureDomains: true` and a `failureDomain` on each of its three groups. Validation runs on every pass of a draft, raises a `Warning` event for each finding under its reason, and writes the first finding into `status.message` with a count of the rest. Three findings are about failure domains.',
        { table: { head: ['Finding', 'When', 'What the message says'], rows: [
          ['`FailureDomainLayout`', 'A document creating a cluster with the mode on, whose domains the control plane would refuse', 'Fewer domains than parity plus two, or unequal worker counts, with the layout spelled out'],
          ['Missing domains', 'The cluster requires fault groups and a group declares none', '`the cluster has failure domains enabled and group <set>/<group> declares none; provisioning holds until each declares one`'],
          ['`TooManyFailureDomains`', 'The labels, with the ones the cluster already maps, exceed 256', 'How many domains the document would bring the cluster to'],
        ] } },
        'The layout is computed from the groups: the workers of every group are counted under the group\'s domain, so two groups with the same `failureDomain` are one domain with their workers combined. A document growing a cluster through `clusterRef` is not held to the layout rule, since the control plane checks it at a fresh activation alone.',
        { steps: [
          'Read `status.message`. A refused layout is named there with its domains and counts.',
          'Check that every group carries `failureDomain` and that the six workers are split two, two, and two. A worker in the wrong cabinet is a label to fix on the node, and then a draft to edit or a run to repeat.',
          'Check the stripe against the domain count. `1+1`, `2+1`, and `4+1` activate on three domains, and any stripe with two parity chunks needs four.',
          'Rename the groups after their cabinets if the generated names are unhelpful, set `cluster.name`, and set `approved: true`.',
        ] },
        'A draft with findings is held in `Draft`. An approved document with findings is not held: approval makes it immutable, so a problem found after it can no longer be edited away, and the document is marked `Failed` with the first finding as its message.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The reviewed draft', file: 'examples/failure-domains/03-clusterdeploymentconfig.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'enableFailureDomains: true', text: 'The mode. It lands on the StorageCluster as an immutable field, so a cluster created without it cannot be converted, and one created with it cannot leave it.' },
          { at: 'parityChunks: 1', text: 'One parity chunk, so three domains. Six nodes also satisfy the stripe\'s own minimum of three.' },
          { at: 'nodeProvisioningBudget: 2', text: 'Two workers in the node-add process at once. With failure domains, the waiting workers are dealt into the domains with the fewest hosts first, so the two in flight are in different cabinets.' },
          { at: 'name: cabinet-a', text: 'The group\'s name, chosen by the reviewer. The run named it `group-1-nvme-2x3.49T` after its hardware, and nothing reads the name back.' },
          { at: 'failureDomain: cabinet-a', text: 'The label the run copied from `cabinet=cabinet-a` on the two workers. One per group, and every worker in the group is in it.' },
          { at: 'worker-0', text: 'The two workers of cabinet A. The layout is counted in workers per domain, across every group that names the domain.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'The refused layout: two cabinets',
      body: [
        'The commissioned layout, six workers in two cabinets of three, is the worked refusal. The document is schema-valid and the API admits it, because nothing about two groups is wrong in itself. Validation refuses it on every pass with `FailureDomainLayout`, and `status.message` on the draft reads, with the layout rendered in domain order:',
        { code: 'failure-domain mode with 1+1 needs at least 3 distinct failure domains (the stripe\'s parity plus two), and the workers are in 2 (cabinet-a: 3, cabinet-b: 3)', title: 'status.message on the two-cabinet draft' },
        'The second refusal is unequal counts. Three cabinets holding three, two, and one worker satisfy the count and fail the balance:',
        { code: 'failure-domain mode needs an equal number of workers in every failure domain when the cluster is activated, and the domains hold cabinet-a: 3, cabinet-b: 2, cabinet-c: 1', title: 'status.message on an unbalanced draft' },
        'Discovery asks the same rule before writing the draft. Run on the two-cabinet fleet with `enableFailureDomains` unset, it writes the draft with the mode off and a `ConfigWritten` event quoting the refusal, so the fleet is deployed as an ordinary cluster. Run with `enableFailureDomains: true`, it writes the mode on and quotes the refusal in the same event, and the draft then carries the finding until it is edited.',
        'There are three ways out, and only one keeps the protection. Moving one node of each cabinet into a third cabinet, or labeling a third one, gives three domains of two. Setting `enableFailureDomains: false` deploys the six nodes with no domain awareness at all. Changing the stripe to `1+0` passes the rule with two domains and survives no failure. Changing the document to one cabinet of six is no layout at all: one domain is fewer than three.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The two-cabinet document, as refused', file: 'examples/failure-domains/04-two-cabinets-refused.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'approved: false', text: 'Left unapproved on purpose. A draft with a finding is held, and an approved document with one is marked `Failed`.' },
          { at: 'parityChunks: 1', text: 'The parity the rule counts from. With `parityChunks: 0`, that is `1+0`, two domains would pass and nothing would be protected.' },
          { at: 'failureDomain: cabinet-b', text: 'The second of two domains. Three workers under each is balanced, and balance is the rule\'s second half, checked only once the count passes.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What the operator does after approval',
      body: [
        'The document moves `Draft`, `Expanding`, `Expanded`, the cluster `Pending`, `Creating`, `Provisioning`, `Activating`, `Online`, and each node `Pending`, `Provisioning`, `Online`, as in chapter 1. Failure-domain mode adds work at three points along the way.',
        '**Before any node is created, the labels are mapped.** In `CreatingNodes` the expansion writes every label the document introduces into the cluster\'s `status.failureDomains`, name by name from the lowest free index, so `cabinet-a` is `0`, `cabinet-b` is `1`, and `cabinet-c` is `2`. The mapping is written first so that no node\'s add runs before the index it sends exists, and an entry is never changed or removed once written, because the control plane has placed data under it.',
        '**Each node carries its label and is held without one.** Every StorageNode gets its group\'s label in `spec.config.failureDomain`. The node controller\'s `CheckingConfig` step holds a node that declares none on a cluster with the mode, with a `FailureDomainMissing` event reading `cluster cabinets requires a fault group and this node declares none; set spec.config.failureDomain`. It holds rather than fails because the field is fillable while empty and immutable once set, so filling it in is enough. When the node is added, the operator sends the index its label maps to, and only to a cluster that has the mode: a cluster created without it refuses an add that names one. A label the mapping does not hold is sent as its number when it is one, and is otherwise left to the control plane to assign.',
        '**Node adds are dealt across the domains.** With a budget of two, the workers waiting for a slot are ranked into the domains with the fewest hosts first, so the per-domain counts stay within one of each other as the cluster fills, and a worker whose label is empty is ranked last.',
        '**Activation waits for the layout.** The activation runs through a StorageClusterOps, and before the start call it reads every node\'s `status.failureDomain`, counts hosts per domain, and asks the activation rule again: at least parity plus two domains, equal counts. Until that holds, the operation raises `FailureDomainNotReady` with the reason and waits rather than failing, which is what lets a cluster whose nodes are still arriving sit in `Activating`. A node that has not reported a domain yet is not counted, and neither is a removed one.',
        'Once the control plane has the node, `status.failureDomain` on the StorageNode is the index it assigned, written as a string, which is not always the one the label asked for. The pool and the StorageClass follow as in chapter 1: `cabinets-default` and `simplyblock-cabinets`.',
        { code: 'oc get stc cabinets -n simplyblock -o jsonpath=\'{.status.failureDomains}\'\n[{"index":0,"name":"cabinet-a"},{"index":1,"name":"cabinet-b"},{"index":2,"name":"cabinet-c"}]\n\noc get sn -n simplyblock -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,WORKER:.spec.workerNode,DOMAIN:.spec.config.failureDomain,ASSIGNED:.status.failureDomain,PHASE:.status.phase', title: 'The mapping and the nodes after expansion' },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Growing and shrinking a cluster with domains',
      body: [
        'The mode is immutable on the cluster, and every later document against it is held to the same field rules: the cluster requires a fault group, so a growth document whose group declares none is refused at validation with the missing-domain message, and a growth document that introduces new labels adds them to `status.failureDomains` at the next free indices, counted against the cap of 256 together with the labels already mapped. The activation layout rule is not applied to growth.',
        'What is applied instead is balance. The control plane refuses an add that would leave the domains differing by more than one host, and it refuses it inside the add task rather than at the request, so a growth that adds three workers adds one per cabinet, and a growth of one worker is dealt into whichever cabinet has the fewest.',
        'Removal has the same rule and a floor. A `StorageNodeOps` with action `Remove` computes in its `Validating` step, from the StorageNode objects, the per-domain host counts the removal would leave, and refuses to proceed when two domains would differ by more than one host or any domain would drop below two. A worker that keeps another storage node of the cluster stays in its domain. The refusal holds `Validating` with `RemovalNotAdmitted` and the reason, with nothing done to the node, and the control plane\'s own admission, which also covers fault-tolerance headroom, is asked after it through its read-only check.',
        { callout: 'In the six-node cluster of this chapter, every domain holds exactly two hosts, so removing any one node would drop its domain to one, below the floor, and the drain is held in `Validating`. A cluster of three cabinets of two cannot shrink. It grows first, to three per cabinet, and then a node can go.', kind: 'warn' },
        'The host-side behavior of the node agent during the add, and how the control plane relocates chunks when a domain is lost, cannot be verified in this repository and are not described here.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'A growth document, one worker per cabinet', file: 'examples/failure-domains/05-growth-clusterdeploymentconfig.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'clusterRef: cabinets', text: 'Names the cluster to add to, in place of `cluster`. The mode is read from the live cluster, so every group below still needs a `failureDomain`.' },
          { at: 'failureDomain: cabinet-c', text: 'A label the cluster already maps keeps its index `2`. A new label, `cabinet-d`, would be mapped to `3` before the first node is created.' },
          { at: 'worker-6', text: 'One worker per cabinet keeps the domains at three each. Three workers in one cabinet would be refused add by add inside the control plane\'s task, once that domain is two ahead.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What goes wrong',
      body: [
        { pitfalls: [
          { symptom: 'The draft is held with `failure-domain mode with 1+1 needs at least 3 distinct failure domains`.', cause: 'The workers are in two domains, which no stripe with a parity chunk accepts.', fix: 'Label a third cabinet and move two workers into it, then edit the draft or run discovery again. `1+0` passes and protects nothing.' },
          { symptom: 'The draft is held with `needs an equal number of workers in every failure domain`.', cause: 'The domains hold different numbers of workers, counted across every group that names the domain.', fix: 'Balance the cabinets in the draft, or leave a worker out of this deployment and add it later with a growth document.' },
          { symptom: 'The run wrote the draft with `enableFailureDomains: false` although the nodes are labeled.', cause: 'Either the run read `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` because `failureDomainLabel` was not set, or some workers lack the label and the run raised `TopologyIncomplete`, or the layout would be refused and the run said so in a `ConfigWritten` event.', fix: 'Read the run\'s events. Set the label key on a new run, label the missing nodes, or fix the layout.' },
          { symptom: 'A StorageNode stays in `Provisioning` with `FailureDomainMissing`.', cause: 'The cluster has the mode and the node\'s `spec.config.failureDomain` is empty, which happens to a document approved with a group that declared none.', fix: 'Set `spec.config.failureDomain` on the node. It is fillable while empty and immutable afterward, and the hold lifts on its own.' },
          { symptom: 'The cluster sits in `Activating` and the StorageClusterOps raises `FailureDomainNotReady`.', cause: 'The nodes that have reported a domain so far are not yet equal per domain, or fewer than three domains have reported.', fix: 'Wait while the remaining nodes are added. The gate holds rather than fails, and lifts once every node has reported.' },
          { symptom: 'A `Remove` operation is held in `Validating` with `RemovalNotAdmitted` and a message about a domain dropping to one host.', cause: 'Every domain must keep at least two hosts, and in a cluster of two per cabinet any removal breaks that.', fix: 'Grow the cluster first, one worker per cabinet, then remove. The hold has a long deadline and nothing was done to the node.' },
          { symptom: 'An approved document is `Failed` with a failure-domain message.', cause: 'It was approved while validation still had a finding. Approval makes the document immutable, so the finding can no longer be edited away.', fix: 'Write a new document with the layout corrected. The failed one is the record of what was refused.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],

  questions: [
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'A fleet of six workers in two cabinets of three asks for failure-domain mode with the default stripe. What does the operator do with the draft?', options: ['Refuses it at validation with `FailureDomainLayout`', 'Approves it, because the two domains are balanced', 'Creates the cluster and lets the control plane refuse the activation', 'Merges the two cabinets into one domain', 'Lowers the stripe to `1+0` so that two domains suffice'], correct: [0], explain: 'A `1+1` stripe needs parity plus two, three, distinct domains. The operator mirrors the control plane\'s activation gate at validation so the refusal lands while the document can still be edited, and it never rewrites the stripe.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which stripes activate on three failure domains of equal size?', options: ['`1+1`', '`2+1`', '`4+1`', '`2+2`', '`4+2`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'Parity plus two: one parity chunk needs three domains, two parity chunks need four. The stripe\'s own node minimum still applies, so `4+1` needs six nodes as well.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these layouts pass `FailureDomainViolation` for a `1+1` stripe?', options: ['Three domains of two workers', 'Three domains of three, two, and one worker', 'Two domains of three workers', 'Four domains of two workers', 'One domain of six workers'], correct: [0, 3], explain: 'At least three domains, equal counts. Unequal counts fail the second half, two domains fail the first, and one domain is fewer still. Four of two is more than enough.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'Why does the rule ask for parity plus two domains rather than parity plus one?', options: ['So that after one domain is lost a later add or remove still has a spare domain to relocate onto', 'Because FoundationDB needs three coordinators', 'Because the control plane counts the management node as a domain', 'So that each domain can hold a journal device', 'Because two domains make the index mapping ambiguous'], correct: [0], explain: 'Parity plus one domains can place a stripe but leaves no spare once a domain is down, and the code\'s comment adds that two domains are not supported at any parity. The other reasons are invented.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'Where does the operator ask the failure-domain layout rule?', options: ['In discovery, before proposing the mode', 'In validation, before approval', 'In the StorageClusterOps, before the activation call', 'In the CSI driver, before each volume is created', 'In the pool controller, before the default pool is written'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The rule is asked three times, each earlier than the control plane would ask it: when the draft is written, on every pass over the draft, and as the activation gate that holds with `FailureDomainNotReady`. Volumes and pools do not ask it.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'What does a `1+0` stripe change about a two-cabinet fleet?', options: ['The layout rule passes, because zero parity plus two is two', 'Chunk placement spreads across both cabinets with one parity chunk', 'The cluster survives the loss of a cabinet', 'Two nodes are the minimum for it', 'The operator refuses `1+0` on a cluster with failure domains'], correct: [0], explain: 'With no parity chunk the rule asks for two domains and two cabinets pass, and nothing is protected: `1+0` survives no failure. Its node minimum is one, and nothing refuses it.' },
    { topic: 'Labels', cat: 'field', text: 'Which of these are valid values of `failureDomain`?', options: ['`cabinet-a`', '`rack_b.east`', '`1`', '`cabinet a`', '`-cabinet`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The field takes the shape of a Kubernetes label value: letters, digits, `-`, `_`, and `.`, starting and ending with a letter or digit, at most 63 characters. A space is refused, and so is a leading hyphen. A number is valid and claims its own index when it is free.' },
    { topic: 'Labels', cat: 'concept', text: 'What is a cabinet to the operator?', options: ['The value of a Node label the discovery run reads into `failureDomain`', 'A kind in `storage.simplyblock.io`', 'A field on the StorageCluster that groups nodes', 'A node set name discovery generates', 'An index the control plane assigns'], correct: [0], explain: 'The operator has no notion of a cabinet. It has failure domains, which are label values, and `cabinet-a` is what this fleet calls one. The index is the control plane\'s side of the mapping.' },
    { topic: 'Labels', cat: 'field', text: 'Which fields carry a failure-domain label rather than an index?', options: ['`NodeGroup.failureDomain` on a deployment config', '`StorageNode.spec.config.failureDomain`', '`StorageCluster.status.failureDomains[].name`', '`StorageNode.status.failureDomain`', '`StorageCluster.status.failureDomains[].index`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The label travels from the group to the node and into the mapping\'s `name`. The mapping\'s `index` and the node\'s `status.failureDomain`, which the node controller writes from the control plane\'s integer, are indices.' },
    { topic: 'Labels', cat: 'concept', text: 'How are the labels `cabinet-a`, `cabinet-b`, and `cabinet-c` indexed on a fresh cluster?', options: ['`0`, `1`, `2` in name order', 'In the order the groups appear in the document', 'Randomly, by the control plane', '`1`, `2`, `3` in name order', 'By the hash of each label'], correct: [0], explain: 'New labels are sorted by name and take the lowest free index each, starting at zero. A numeric label claims its own number first when that number is free.' },
    { topic: 'Labels', cat: 'field', text: 'Which statements about `StorageNode.spec.config.failureDomain` are true?', options: ['It is immutable once set', 'It can be filled in while empty', 'The operator sends it to the control plane as a label', 'A node without it on a cluster with the mode is held, not failed', 'It is required on every StorageNode'], correct: [0, 1, 3], explain: 'The field is fillable and then frozen, which is what makes a hold the right response to its absence. The control plane receives the index the cluster maps the label to, and the field is required only when the cluster has `enableFailureDomains`.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'What does `spec.discover.failureDomainLabel` do?', options: ['Names the Node label the run reads each worker\'s domain from', 'Sets the value every worker\'s domain gets', 'Turns failure-domain mode on', 'Selects which workers the run inspects', 'Renames the groups after their domains'], correct: [0], explain: 'It replaces `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` as the source of the value, which is copied verbatim. The mode is `enableFailureDomains`, worker selection is `workers` and `nodeSelector`, and the groups are named after their hardware.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'A run with `enableFailureDomains` unset inspects six labeled workers in two cabinets. What does it write?', options: ['A draft with the mode off and a `ConfigWritten` event quoting the refusal', 'A draft with the mode on and a `FailureDomainLayout` finding', 'No draft, the run fails', 'A draft with the two cabinets merged into one domain', 'A draft with `1+0` chosen for the stripe'], correct: [0], explain: 'Unset leaves the decision to the run, which proposes the mode on only when the layout would activate. Two domains would not, so the draft carries the mode off and the event on the run says why. The mode on with a finding is what `enableFailureDomains: true` produces.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these does a run with `enableFailureDomains: false` do?', options: ['Reads no failure-domain label at all', 'Groups the fleet by hardware alone', 'Writes `enableFailureDomains: false` on the draft', 'Refuses workers that carry a domain label', 'Fails on a fleet in a single zone'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'False turns the mode off and reads no topology, so workers in different zones with the same hardware are one group. Nothing is refused and nothing fails.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Four of six workers carry `cabinet=...` and two do not. What does the run report?', options: ['`TopologyIncomplete`, naming the two, and the draft proposes the mode off', '`FailureDomainInvalid`, naming the two', 'Nothing, the two are placed in domain `0`', 'The two are left out of the draft', 'The run fails before writing a draft'], correct: [0], explain: 'A fleet where some workers have a label and others do not is reported under `TopologyIncomplete`, the unlabeled workers get no domain, and the draft does not enable the mode. `FailureDomainInvalid` is a label whose value the field cannot hold.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Six identical machines in three cabinets are discovered. How many groups does the draft hold?', options: ['One, because the hardware is identical', 'Three, one per domain', 'Six, one per worker', 'Two, split by size', 'As many as the run\'s `nodeSelector` matches'], correct: [1], explain: 'The failure domain is part of the group signature, so identical hardware in different domains is different groups. Each group carries its domain in `failureDomain`.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'Which of these are chart values?', options: ['`discovery.enableFailureDomains`', '`discovery.failureDomainLabel`', '`discovery.enableControlPlaneNodes`', '`discovery.cabinetLabel`', '`discovery.enablePartitionedDevices`'], correct: [0, 2, 4], explain: 'The chart carries the mode flag and the flags chapter 1 used, and no value for the label key, so a fleet labeling cabinets writes its run by hand with the chart\'s run turned off.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'field', text: 'Where does the mode live on a deployment config, and what happens to it?', options: ['`spec.cluster.enableFailureDomains`, expanded into the StorageCluster\'s immutable field of the same name', '`spec.enableFailureDomains`, read by the run only', 'On each group, as `failureDomains: true`', '`spec.nodeSets[].enableFailureDomains`', 'It is derived from whether any group carries `failureDomain`'], correct: [0], explain: 'The template field becomes the cluster\'s field, which is immutable, so the choice is made once. A group carrying a label does not turn the mode on by itself.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about the layout check at validation are true?', options: ['Workers are counted per `failureDomain` across every group that names it', 'A growth document with `clusterRef` is not held to it', 'Each node set is one domain', 'The labels are read off the Node objects again', 'Devices are counted per domain'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Two groups naming the same domain are one domain with their workers combined, and the document is the source, not the nodes. The control plane checks the layout at a fresh activation alone, so a growth document is not held to it.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'A draft with the mode on has one group with no `failureDomain`. Which of these are true?', options: ['Validation reports that provisioning would hold until each group declares one', 'The layout rule is not evaluated, because the layout is not known', 'The draft is expanded with that group\'s nodes in domain `0`', 'The draft is deleted', 'The group is dropped from the expansion'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The missing domain is its own finding, and the layout check steps aside when a group has none. Nothing guesses a domain and nothing drops a group.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'A reviewer sets `approved: true` on a draft that still carries `FailureDomainLayout`. What happens?', options: ['The document is marked `Failed` with the finding as its message', 'The document is held in `Draft` until the layout is fixed', 'The cluster is created and activation waits', 'The approval is refused by the webhook', 'The operator edits the layout to pass'], correct: [0], explain: 'Approval makes the document immutable, so a finding can no longer be edited away, and holding forever would say less than failing. A fresh document is the way forward.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'field', text: 'How many failure domains may one cluster map, and what refuses more?', options: ['256, refused at validation with `TooManyFailureDomains`', '64, refused by the schema of `status.failureDomains`', '3, one per parity chunk plus two', 'Unlimited', '16, refused by the control plane at activation'], correct: [0], explain: '`status.failureDomains` holds at most 256 entries, and a document that would take its cluster past that, counting the labels already mapped, is refused before approval rather than stalling after it.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'action', text: 'In which order does the expansion do these?', options: ['Write the labels into `status.failureDomains`, then create the StorageNodes', 'Create the StorageNodes, then map whatever labels they carry', 'Activate the cluster, then map the labels', 'Map the labels only when the first node is added', 'Map the labels on the control plane first'], correct: [0], explain: 'The mapping is written in `CreatingNodes` before any node is created, so no add runs before the index it sends exists.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about `status.failureDomains` on the StorageCluster are true?', options: ['An entry is never changed once written', 'An entry is never removed once written', 'A growth document adds entries for new labels', 'The control plane writes it', 'It is cleared when the cluster goes `Online`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The control plane has placed data under each index, so the mapping only ever grows, and it grows when a document introduces a label. The operator writes it, in `CreatingNodes`.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about `FailureDomainMissing` on a StorageNode are true?', options: ['The cluster has the mode and the node declares no `spec.config.failureDomain`', 'The node is held, and filling the field in lifts the hold', 'The node\'s label is not in the cluster\'s mapping', 'The Node object lost its label', 'The node is in a domain that is already full'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'It is the `CheckingConfig` hold: the field is required by the cluster and empty on the node, and the field is fillable while empty. A label not in the mapping is sent as its number when it is one, and otherwise left to the control plane, which is no hold at all.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'action', text: 'What does the operator send to the control plane for a node\'s failure domain?', options: ['The index the cluster\'s mapping holds for the label', 'The label itself', 'The label, but only to a cluster without the mode', 'The number, when the label is a number not in the mapping', 'Nothing, when the cluster was created without the mode'], correct: [0, 3, 4], explain: 'The control plane\'s field is an integer. The mapped index is sent, an unmapped numeric label is sent as its number, an unmapped other label is left for the control plane to assign, and a cluster without the mode refuses an add that names one, so nothing is sent to it.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are true of `status.failureDomain` on a StorageNode?', options: ['It is the index the control plane assigned, written as a string', 'It can differ from what `spec.config.failureDomain` asked for', 'The activation gate reads it to count hosts per domain', 'It is the label copied from the group', 'It is set before the node is added'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The node controller writes the control plane\'s integer, which is what was assigned rather than what was requested. The StorageClusterOps counts hosts per domain from it, skipping nodes that have not reported one.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'With `nodeProvisioningBudget: 2` on this chapter\'s cluster, which two workers are added first?', options: ['Two workers from different cabinets', 'The two workers of cabinet A', 'Whichever two are first by name, regardless of cabinet', 'One worker only, because failure domains force a budget of one', 'The two with the most devices'], correct: [0], explain: 'With failure domains the waiting workers are dealt into the domains with the fewest hosts first, so the per-domain counts stay within one of each other as the cluster fills.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'The cluster sits in `Activating` and the StorageClusterOps raises `FailureDomainNotReady`. Which of these should be done?', options: ['Delete and re-create the StorageClusterOps', 'Edit `enableFailureDomains` on the cluster', 'Approve the document again', 'Relabel the Node objects', 'Set `spec.config.failureDomain` on every node again'], correct: [], explain: 'None of them. The gate holds while nodes are still arriving and lifts on its own once every node has reported a domain and the counts are equal. The cluster field is immutable, the document is immutable, and the node field is immutable once set.' },
    { topic: 'Growth and removal', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these does validation apply to a growth document against a cluster with the mode?', options: ['Every group must declare a `failureDomain`', 'The 256-domain cap, counting the labels the cluster already maps', 'The activation layout rule', 'Equal worker counts across the document\'s groups', 'A fresh `enableFailureDomains` on the document'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The cluster requires fault groups, and the mapping has a cap. The activation layout rule is checked at a fresh activation alone, so growth is not held to it, and the mode is read from the live cluster rather than restated.' },
    { topic: 'Growth and removal', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about growing a cluster in failure-domain mode are true?', options: ['Domains may differ by at most one host', 'An add that breaks the balance is refused inside the add task, after the request was accepted', 'Every domain must hold the same number of hosts after every add', 'Only one domain may grow per document', 'Growth is refused while the mode is on'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Equality is required at activation only. An add that would put a domain two hosts ahead is refused by the control plane inside the task rather than at the request, which is why the operator deals waiting workers into the domains with the fewest hosts.' },
    { topic: 'The rule', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are true of the failure-domain layout rule the operator applies?', options: ['It mirrors sbcli\'s activation gate', 'It counts workers per domain, not devices', 'It is asked before approval', 'It requires at least the stripe\'s parity plus two domains', 'It requires the same number of workers in every domain'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], explain: 'All five. The rule is a mirror of the control plane\'s, over workers, asked at discovery, at validation, and before activation, and it has the two halves the refusal messages name.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these does a discovery run raise as events on itself?', options: ['`TopologyIncomplete`, naming workers without the label when others have it', '`FailureDomainInvalid`, naming a worker whose value the field cannot hold', '`ConfigWritten`, with the notes on why the mode is on or off', '`DeviceDeclined`, for each device the run refused', '`FailureDomainLayout`, for a layout the control plane would refuse'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The first four are the run\'s events. `FailureDomainLayout` is a validation finding raised on the document, and the run quotes the same refusal in its `ConfigWritten` note instead.' },
    { topic: 'After approval', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these happen to a label a document introduces?', options: ['It gets an index in `status.failureDomains` before any node is created', 'It is sorted by name with the other new labels', 'It takes the lowest free index, unless it is a number that is free', 'It keeps its index for as long as the cluster exists', 'It is sent to the control plane as text'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The mapping is written in `CreatingNodes`, in name order from the lowest free index, with a numeric label claiming its own number first, and it is never changed afterward. The control plane receives the index, never the text.' },
    { topic: 'Growth and removal', cat: 'concept', text: 'A `Remove` operation is written for `worker-3` in this chapter\'s cluster of three cabinets of two. What happens?', options: ['The drain is held in `Validating` with `RemovalNotAdmitted`, because cabinet B would drop to one host', 'The node is removed and cabinet B runs with one host', 'The cluster is rebalanced to two cabinets', 'The operation fails immediately', 'The node is shut down, and then the control plane refuses'], correct: [0], explain: 'The removal rule requires at least two hosts in every domain after the removal, and the operator computes the counts before the shutdown, so the node is untouched. The hold has a long deadline and waits for a decision.' },
    { topic: 'Growth and removal', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these are checked by the operator itself in a drain\'s `Validating` step?', options: ['No two domains differ by more than one host after the removal', 'No domain drops below two hosts', 'Whether another node of the cluster keeps the worker in its domain', 'Fault-tolerance headroom', 'Active control-plane tasks'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The balance and the floor are computed from the StorageNode objects, with a multi-node worker counted once. Headroom, replica relocation, and active tasks are the control plane\'s own admission, asked through its read-only check afterward.' },
    { topic: 'Growth and removal', cat: 'field', text: 'Which of these can be changed on a running cluster in failure-domain mode?', options: ['`StorageCluster.spec.enableFailureDomains`', 'A StorageNode\'s `spec.config.failureDomain` that is already set', 'An entry of `status.failureDomains`', 'The approved document\'s groups', 'The stripe'], correct: [], explain: 'None of them. The mode and the stripe are immutable on the cluster, a set domain is frozen on the node, mapping entries are never changed, and an approved document is immutable.' },
  ],

  tasks: [
    {
      title: 'A discovery run over labeled cabinets',
      scenario: 'The six workers carry `cabinet=cabinet-a`, `cabinet=cabinet-b`, or `cabinet=cabinet-c`. Write the run that reads that label and states failure-domain mode on.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: {{kind}}\nmetadata:\n  name: cabinets\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  action: {{action}}\n  discover:\n    {{labelField}}: cabinet\n    enableFailureDomains: {{mode}}\n    enablePartitionedDevices: true\n',
      blanks: {
        kind: { accept: ['OperatorOps'], hint: 'the operator-level operation kind, short name `oops`' },
        action: { accept: ['Discover'], hint: 'its only action today' },
        labelField: { accept: ['failureDomainLabel'], hint: 'the field naming the Node label the run reads, in place of the zone label' },
        mode: { accept: ['true'], hint: 'stated rather than left to the run' },
      },
      explain: 'An `OperatorOps` with `action: Discover` writes the draft. `failureDomainLabel` replaces `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` as the source of each worker\'s domain, and `enableFailureDomains: true` states the mode instead of leaving the run to decide it.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The reviewed three-cabinet draft',
      scenario: 'Complete the draft so that it is expanded into a cluster named `cabinets` in failure-domain mode with the default stripe, six workers in three cabinets of two.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: ClusterDeploymentConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: discovered-cabinets\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  approved: true\n  environment: OpenShift\n  cluster:\n    name: cabinets\n    maxSubsystemCount: 50\n    vcpuCount: 8\n    stripe:\n      dataChunks: 1\n      parityChunks: {{parity}}\n    {{modeField}}: true\n  nodeSets:\n    - name: cabinets\n      groups:\n        - name: cabinet-a\n          {{domainField}}: cabinet-a\n          workers: [worker-0, worker-1]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n        - name: cabinet-b\n          failureDomain: {{domainB}}\n          workers: [worker-2, worker-3]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n        - name: cabinet-c\n          failureDomain: cabinet-c\n          workers: [{{workersC}}]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n',
      blanks: {
        parity: { accept: ['1'], hint: 'the default stripe, which three domains carry' },
        modeField: { accept: ['enableFailureDomains'], hint: 'the template field that becomes the cluster\'s immutable mode' },
        domainField: { accept: ['failureDomain'], hint: 'one label per group' },
        domainB: { accept: ['cabinet-b'], hint: 'the label the two workers of cabinet B carry' },
        workersC: { accept: ['worker-4, worker-5', '/worker-4\\s*,\\s*worker-5/', '/worker-5\\s*,\\s*worker-4/'], hint: 'the remaining two workers, so every domain holds two' },
      },
      explain: '`1+1` needs three domains, `enableFailureDomains` on the template lands on the cluster as its immutable mode, and every group names its domain with `failureDomain`. Two workers in cabinet C keeps the counts equal, which activation requires.',
    },
    {
      title: 'A growth document, one worker per cabinet',
      scenario: 'Three more workers, `worker-6` in cabinet A, `worker-7` in cabinet B, and `worker-8` in cabinet C, join the cluster `cabinets` later. Write the document that adds them without unbalancing the domains.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: ClusterDeploymentConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: cabinets-growth\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  approved: true\n  environment: OpenShift\n  {{refField}}: {{cluster}}\n  nodeSets:\n    - name: cabinets-growth\n      groups:\n        - name: cabinet-a\n          failureDomain: cabinet-a\n          workers: [worker-6]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n        - name: cabinet-b\n          failureDomain: {{domainB}}\n          workers: [worker-7]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n        - name: cabinet-c\n          failureDomain: cabinet-c\n          workers: [{{workerC}}]\n          mgmtInterface: br-ex\n          dataInterfaces: [br-ex]\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:c1:00.0"]\n',
      blanks: {
        refField: { accept: ['clusterRef'], hint: 'names an existing cluster, in place of `cluster`' },
        cluster: { accept: ['cabinets'], hint: 'the cluster this chapter created' },
        domainB: { accept: ['cabinet-b'], hint: 'a label the cluster already maps, which keeps its index' },
        workerC: { accept: ['worker-8'], hint: 'the worker that keeps cabinet C level with the others' },
      },
      explain: '`clusterRef` adds to an existing cluster, whose immutable mode is why every group still needs a `failureDomain`. The labels are ones the cluster maps already, so the mapping gains nothing, and one worker per cabinet keeps the domains within one host of each other, which the control plane requires of every add.',
    },
  ],
});
